@TITLE@ QilinRecomp for Windows (64-bit)
========================================================

@TITLE@ (@STUDIO@), recompiled to run natively: the game's own
program translated into C++ and compiled for your PC, drawn with Vulkan.

No game data is included. You need your own copy of the game: @RELEASE@,
its disc image extracted to a folder (for example with extract-xiso or
xdvdfs). That gives a folder holding default.xbe and the folders bundles,
demand, xwavebank and the rest.

This is a work in progress: the game plays, and some things are still
missing or wrong. Changes: CHANGELOG.md on the project's page.


Playing
-------
1. Unzip the download anywhere. Building the game needs about 2 GB free.
2. Open the folder and double-click "Play @TITLE@.exe".
3. Click "Choose your disc folder" and pick the folder you extracted the
   disc into (the one holding default.xbe), or drag the folder onto the
   window. It tells you straight away if it's the wrong game or another
   release.
4. Wait for the first build: a few minutes (up to 15 on a slower PC). Your
   game files never leave your PC.
5. Click Play. The window hides while the game runs. "Add a desktop
   shortcut" puts Play @TITLE@ on your desktop.

After that, Play @TITLE@.exe remembers your disc folder (even in a new
download folder), and Play starts the game straight away. After an update
it builds the game again first, recompiling only what changed.

The game's output is in work\run.log and work\run-errors.log, and the
build's in work\build.log. Send them when reporting a problem. If the
picture is wrong (something flickers, a shadow or an effect looks off),
press F9 while it shows: the next 12 frames and what the game drew in them
are saved to a file in the data folder's captures folder (the game says
where). Send that file too.

If Windows says "Windows protected your PC": More info, then Run anyway.
(The download isn't signed.) Or before unpacking, right-click the zip,
Properties, tick Unblock, OK.

Needs: Windows 10 (version 1803 or later) or 11, 64-bit, and a graphics
driver with Vulkan 1.3 (any recent NVIDIA, AMD or Intel driver).


Controls
--------
Any controller Windows or SDL knows (Xbox, PlayStation, Switch, generic),
as the Xbox controller, or the keyboard as controller 1:

  Xbox controller      Keyboard
  left stick           W A S D
  right stick          numeric keypad 8 4 5 6
  D-pad                arrows
  A B X Y              Space or K, L, J, I
  White, Black         Q, E   (left and right shoulder buttons)
  triggers             Left Shift, Right Shift or O
  Start, Back          Enter, Backspace or Tab
  stick clicks         F, H

F1 or Esc, or Back and Start together on a controller: the settings menu
(the game pauses): the display (full screen, the aspect ratio, the HUD on
wide screens, the render resolution, SMAA, AMD FSR 1, a CRT look, vsync,
the frame rate, a frame rate counter on F3, texture filtering), the
controls (rumble, the keyboard's keys, set by pressing them) and the game
(volume, stereo or 5.1, the sound device, the language).
F11 or Alt+Enter: full screen. The window can be resized: the picture keeps
its shape.

Saves and settings are kept in %LOCALAPPDATA%\qilin\@GAME@ (the settings
in ghoulies.ini, which can be edited by hand too), with the disc folder you
chose and the build's cache of compiled files (build-cache).


What's in here
--------------
Play @TITLE@.exe  chooses your disc folder, builds the game (once)
                             and starts it
kit\qilin.exe       the recompiler: turns the disc's @BOOT@ into C++
kit\lib, include    the prebuilt rest of the game: its runtime, the Xbox's
                    kernel, Direct3D and DirectSound replaced natively, the
                    renderer (Vulkan), the window, controls and sound (SDL3),
                    the settings menu (Dear ImGui)
kit\toolchain       LLVM-MinGW, a C++ compiler for Windows (Clang)
kit\files.txt       every file this release holds (see below)
work\               made when building: the game (@EXE@), built from your
                    disc, the recompiled code, the build's files and the logs
LICENSES\           the licences of everything included

Source, changes and issues: https://github.com/@REPO@


Updates and recovery
--------------------
"Check for updates" in Play @TITLE@.exe asks GitHub for a newer
release (it sends nothing about you or your PC). If there is one, it
downloads it, unpacks it beside this folder and swaps in the new kit and
launcher, then starts again and builds the game for the new version. If
anything fails on the way, the old version is left as it was. You can also
unzip a new download anywhere, even over this folder: it finds your disc
folder and the cache by itself, and at its first start it removes files the
new kit no longer has (anything in kit\ or LICENSES\ that kit\files.txt
doesn't name). Downloads before the launcher had "Play Ghoulies.bat": over
one of those, delete that file (it no longer works) and choose your disc
folder once more. Your saves and settings are never in this folder, so
nothing of yours is touched.

The game is compiled for this PC's processor; moved to another PC, it is
built again. Compiled files come from the cache when nothing they're made
from has changed. A failed build leaves the working game as it was; the
one before a new build is kept as work\@EXE@.previous until the new one has
run and closed normally. "Build again from scratch" compiles everything
anew.

Before changing an existing title-save file, the runtime keeps five copies
under the data folder: save-history\partition1\<relative file>\1.bin
through 5.bin (1 is the newest). Cache partitions are left out, and the
game doesn't see these copies. If a copy can't be made, the write is
refused. To restore one: quit the game, keep a separate copy of the current
partition1, and copy the chosen snapshot back to partition1\<relative
file>. Restore related files together if the game saved them as a group.
This is file-level history, not a whole-disk transaction.
